$DIR/main.p:12:2: cannot store &x in package variable g, which outlives x
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:11).
    When f returns, x's memory is reclaimed, but package variables live for the
    whole program, so package variable g would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value instead of a pointer to it, changing the type of g to match.
    This copies x, or moves it if it is an owner:
        g = x
    Or allocate x and store its owner, an owned[int], which moves into g:
        g = new(int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
